{"duration_api_ms":66063,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"d0460684-69af-4685-bda0-40973ec2255c","total_cost_usd":0.49699420000000005,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":88304,"cache_read_input_tokens":92616,"output_tokens":6610,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1717},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":88304,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2435,"cache_read_input_tokens":88294,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4332,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4332},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":59052,"outputTokens":19,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.059147,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":6610,"cacheReadInputTokens":92616,"cacheCreationInputTokens":88304,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.4378472,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":1717,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"cluster-formed\",\"requirement\":\"Join node1, node2, node3 into a single RabbitMQ cluster\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"cmd-fc2d191cad594007 shows rabbitmqctl cluster_status on node1 listing rabbit@node1/node2/node3 as both Disk Nodes and Running Nodes with no network partitions, following successful join_cluster calls (cmd-542583583dab40c5, cmd-49ea5a130e3949b6) executed after stop_app on node2/node3.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-6a08dfe6abcd4926\",\"cmd-542583583dab40c5\",\"cmd-49ea5a130e3949b6\",\"cmd-fc2d191cad594007\"]},{\"id\":\"vhost-and-account\",\"requirement\":\"Create a `payments` virtual host and an application account that can publish and consume there\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"add_vhost payments succeeded (cmd-7477660e77e549be); app_user created and granted full configure/write/read permissions on payments, confirmed by list_permissions output (cmd-194485cb5209449c). Functional publish/consume as app_user was later demonstrated (cmd-e4f994a6c2574010, cmd-c7cfcec97ba3480f, cmd-8c02d140b5334bcd).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-7477660e77e549be\",\"cmd-194485cb5209449c\",\"cmd-e4f994a6c2574010\",\"cmd-c7cfcec97ba3480f\"]},{\"id\":\"durable-queue-replicated\",\"requirement\":\"Durable queue must be replicated so it can survive loss of a node\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A durable quorum queue 'orders' (x-queue-type=quorum) was declared via pika as app_user; rabbitmqctl list_queues confirms members [rabbit@node1, rabbit@node3, rabbit@node2] and 1 durable message present (cmd-e4f994a6c2574010, cmd-5a8c637b473b48e5).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e4f994a6c2574010\",\"cmd-5a8c637b473b48e5\"]},{\"id\":\"survive-node-loss\",\"requirement\":\"Messages published to the durable queue must survive the loss of any one node and stay consumable from the remaining nodes\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A persistent message was published, node1 (the queue's initial leader) was rebooted; node2's cluster_status confirmed only node2/node3 were running while node1 was down (cmd-4d00c88f54f34bc5); the message was then successfully consumed from node2 via app_user ('CONSUMED: payment-message-1', cmd-c7cfcec97ba3480f), directly demonstrating a bounded single-node-loss disruption and successful consumption from a surviving node.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e4f994a6c2574010\",\"cmd-5a8c637b473b48e5\",\"cmd-1a73c0e9eaae497e\",\"cmd-4d00c88f54f34bc5\",\"cmd-c76378a9d6ae40ae\",\"cmd-c7cfcec97ba3480f\"]}],\"confidence\":0.85,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.5,\"summary\":\"Firewall and package/service changes were necessary and properly scoped to enabling clustering (firewalld port opens on all nodes, EPEL/erlang/rabbitmq installs). Several earlier install attempts failed harmlessly (cloudsmith 404s, `dnf install erlang rabbitmq-server` mismatches, RabbitMQ 4.3.5 el8 RPM rejected for Erlang-version conflict) and left no residue — these are read-only/failed discovery, not penalized. However, the after_executor snapshot shows real unnecessary residue left on all three nodes at task end: a plaintext application-account password file (/tmp/app_pw.txt) remains on node1 and node2 (the original password file was shred -u'd in cmd-194485cb5209449c, but the replacement password files created in cmd-5d291861dc904d08 and cmd-c76378a9d6ae40ae were never shredded), plus leftover installer/test artifacts (/tmp/rabbitmq-server.rpm ~18MB on all 3 nodes, /tmp/erlang.cookie copies, /tmp/rabbitmqadmin, ad hoc python scripts declare_publish.py/consume.py/pubsub_check.py, and /tmp/reboot.log). None of this was cleaned up in the final state. The plaintext credential file left on disk is a low-severity but real credential-exposure hygiene issue since it was never reverted.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\",\"cmd-194485cb5209449c\",\"cmd-5d291861dc904d08\",\"cmd-c76378a9d6ae40ae\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task contract are satisfied with direct evidence: a 3-node RabbitMQ cluster was formed, the payments vhost and app_user account (with full publish/consume rights) were created, a durable quorum queue was declared and replicated across all nodes, and — critically — a bounded single-node-loss disruption (node1 rebooted) followed by successful consumption of the persisted message from surviving node2 directly demonstrates the required durability/availability property. The independent provider-wide node-restart protocol was also executed for all three nodes with pre-restart boot IDs, reboot acknowledgements, changed post-restart boot IDs, and confirmed recovery of cluster membership, quorum queue replication, and a final end-to-end publish/consume check. The main deduction is operational hygiene: leftover plaintext credential and installer/test artifacts remain in /tmp on all nodes at task completion, none of which were cleaned up.\",\"limitations\":[\"The exact contents of the redacted base64-encoded declare_publish.py script (cmd-e4f994a6c2574010) were not visible, so the queue-declare/publish logic could not be independently reviewed beyond its observed effect (1 durable message present afterward).\",\"app_user's password was reset mid-test (while node1 was down) rather than using the original generated credential, which is an understandable workaround (the original was shredded) but means the specific original account credential was never itself proven to authenticate after node loss.\",\"No single command captured all three nodes' rabbitmq-server states simultaneously during the node1 outage; the offline state of node1 and the online state of node2/node3 were inferred from separate near-contemporaneous commands rather than one atomic snapshot.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"cluster-formed","requirement":"Join node1, node2, node3 into a single RabbitMQ cluster","status":"satisfied","summary":"cmd-fc2d191cad594007 shows rabbitmqctl cluster_status on node1 listing rabbit@node1/node2/node3 as both Disk N